    slug: 'executive-inbox-delegation-boundary', title: 'Executive inbox delegation: separate sorting from business commitment', excerpt: 'Research on the boundary between inbox preparation, sensitive information handling, and replies that commit an owner or business.', published: '2026-08-17', image: '/images/assistant-maya.jpg', imageAlt: 'Executive assistant reviewing a structured inbox on a laptop',
    keyStats: [{value:'2',label:'Public control sources compared',source:'NIST and FTC'},{value:'3',label:'Inbox actions examined',source:'Study scope'},{value:'0',label:'Default authority to send commitments',source:'Boundary finding'}],
    takeaways: ['Least privilege reduces exposure but does not classify every message.','Sorting, drafting, and sending are separate authority decisions.','The sample should include sensitive and ambiguous messages.'],
    sections: [{heading:'Research question and method',body:'When can executive inbox work move to a remote assistant without turning message sorting into unauthorized judgment? I compared NIST small-business cybersecurity guidance with FTC privacy guidance, then mapped each source to three observable actions: label, draft, and send. The unit was a message plus its requested business outcome; the study did not measure a particular company or assistant.'},{heading:'Evidence and analysis',body:'NIST frames least privilege as limiting access to what a person needs, while FTC guidance treats collection and protection of personal information as a business responsibility. Together, those sources support a narrow inbox lane, but neither source says that a message is safe because it is short or routine. A label can be prepared from defined examples. A draft can be held for review. Sending can create a promise, disclose information, or change a relationship, so it needs a separate approval rule.'},{heading:'Limitations',body:'The cited guidance does not identify which messages are safe in a particular executive inbox, establish an accuracy rate, or replace legal, privacy, or security advice. Classification also cannot reliably infer urgency, privilege, fraud, or relationship risk from text alone.'},{heading:'Conclusion',body:'Start with labels and held drafts. Review errors by category—payment, access recovery, private personnel matters, commitments, and unclear requests—before considering any send permission. Accurate preparation is evidence for a better scope, not evidence that business authority should transfer.'}],
    tables: [{caption:'Inbox action boundary',columns:['Action','Evidence required','Owner decision'],rows:[['Label','Approved examples and minimum access','Review classification errors'],['Draft','Source message and approved voice','Approve wording and claim'],['Send','Explicit recipient and authority','Keep commitments and disclosures with owner']]}],
    sources:[{name:'NIST, Small Business Cybersecurity: Non-Employer Firms',url:'https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/cswp/50/ipd'},{name:'FTC, Protecting Personal Information: A Guide for Business',url:'https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/protecting-personal-information-guide-business'}], related:[{label:'Research library',href:'/research'},{label:'Executive assistance service',href:'/services/executive-assistance'}]
