    keyStats:[{value:'2',label:'Scheduling references compared',source:'IANA and Google'},{value:'4',label:'Fields in a reliable handoff',source:'Study scope'},{value:'1',label:'Named owner for conflicts',source:'Boundary finding'}], takeaways:['A displayed time is not enough without a zone and date context.','Booking options and booking authority should be separated.','Coverage should be judged by resolved exceptions, not overlap hours.'],
    sections:[{heading:'Research question and method',body:'Does time-zone overlap improve calendar support when the handoff record is incomplete or booking authority is unclear? I compared IANA time-zone documentation with Google Calendar guidance and examined a scheduling request as the unit of analysis. The scope was online coordination across local and Philippines work hours, not a productivity or staffing experiment.'},{heading:'Evidence and analysis',body:'IANA documents changing offsets and regional rules; Google explains that calendar sharing and event settings control what collaborators can see and change. This makes a time-zone label a data field, not a complete handoff. A remote assistant can prepare options when the request records date, zone, attendees, duration, and preference. Rescheduling a sensitive meeting, resolving a conflict, or committing an executive requires the owner’s decision even when the assistant has overlapping hours.'},{heading:'Limitations',body:'Time-zone references cannot measure the importance of a meeting, the cost of a missed handoff, or whether an attendee supplied reliable availability. Calendar permissions also vary by account configuration and do not establish a universal access design.'},{heading:'Conclusion',body:'Measure calendar support by the proportion of requests that carry complete context and by the number of exceptions correctly escalated. A short overlap window can work when the record is precise; a long overlap window cannot repair missing authority or ambiguous time data.'}],
